export const CAUSAL_MODULES = [
  {
    id: 'pot_outcomes',
    title: 'Potential Outcomes Framework',
    subtitle: 'Rubin causal model, ATE/ATT, fundamental problem of causal inference',
    difficulty: 'foundational',
    estimatedMin: 29,
    tags: ['causal inference', 'potential outcomes', 'ATE', 'Rubin'],
    summary: `The fundamental problem of causal inference is that you can only see one world: a person either got the treatment or they did not. You can never observe the counterfactual. The potential outcomes framework names this explicitly: Y(1) is the outcome under treatment, Y(0) under control. The individual treatment effect Y(1) - Y(0) is never observable — one of the two is always missing. This is not a data collection problem that more data solves; it is a logical impossibility. ATE = E[Y(1) - Y(0)] is what we can estimate under assumptions, by averaging across the population. ATT = E[Y(1) - Y(0) | T=1] averages only over those who received treatment. Making the assumptions explicit is the entire discipline: ignorability requires that all confounders are measured; SUTVA requires that one unit's treatment does not affect another's outcome; overlap requires that every type of unit could have been in either group. Each assumption is a claim about the world that can be wrong — and cannot be verified from the data used to make the estimate.`,
    keyPoints: [
      `**For unit i, you observe Y_i(1) if T_i=1 or Y_i(0) if T_i=0 — never both.** Individual treatment effects are never identified. Every causal estimate depends on assumptions about the missing potential outcome, and those assumptions cannot be tested from the same data used to estimate the effect. The assumptions are either argued from domain knowledge or embedded in the study design.`,
      `**ATE = E[Y(1) - Y(0)] is the effect for a randomly drawn person from the population.** ATT = E[Y(1) - Y(0) | T=1] is the effect specifically for people who received treatment. These differ when treatment takers are systematically different from non-takers. ATT answers "did our discount campaign work for the users we sent it to?" ATE answers "what would happen if we rolled it out to everyone?" Using ATT to justify universal rollout is an estimand error.`,
      `**The naive comparison E[Y|T=1] - E[Y|T=0] equals ATE plus selection bias: E[Y(0)|T=1] - E[Y(0)|T=0].** People who select into treatment were already different before treatment. Observational studies of exercise and heart disease consistently overstate exercise benefits because health-conscious people both exercise more and eat better — the comparison conflates cause and prior differences in health behavior.`,
      `**Ignorability (unconfoundedness): (Y(0), Y(1)) ⊥ T | X.** Conditional on measured covariates X, treatment assignment is independent of potential outcomes. This is the identifying assumption for observational causal inference — and the one you cannot test from data. You must argue from domain knowledge that you measured every variable that jointly predicts both who selected into treatment and what the outcome would be. Any unmeasured common cause is a threat to this assumption that no estimator can fix.`,
      `**SUTVA requires two things: no interference (unit i's outcome depends only on i's own treatment, not on j's) and no hidden versions of treatment (one well-defined treatment level).** Social app experiments routinely violate the first: treating one user changes the behavior of untreated friends who see their activity. The naive estimate conflates the direct effect of treatment with the spillover effect on untreated units.`,
      `**Overlap (positivity): 0 < P(T=1|X) < 1 for all covariate values in the population.** Every type of unit must have some positive probability of appearing in either the treatment or control group. When a region of covariate space has only treated units and no control units, any causal estimate in that region is pure extrapolation — it depends entirely on model assumptions rather than empirical comparison of units that received different treatments.`,
      `**A well-run A/B test makes T independent of all potential outcomes by construction — randomization guarantees ignorability without any assumption about which covariates you measured.** In observational studies, ignorability must be argued and is almost always uncertain: every unmeasured common cause of treatment and outcome is a threat you are hoping is small enough to ignore.`,
      `**ATE and ATT can differ substantially and the difference has policy consequences.** Heavy users who self-select into a new product feature may benefit most (high ATT). Light users who would be reached by a universal rollout may barely benefit (low ATE). Defining the estimand — who exactly is the effect for? — is the first design decision and determines which estimate is the right answer to the policy question.`,
    ],
    checkQuestions: [
      {
        q: `Observed data shows users who saw an ad (T=1) had 20% higher conversion than users who did not (T=0). Can you conclude the ad caused 20% lift? What would you need to make a causal claim?`,
        a: `No. The 20% is a naive comparison that includes selection bias: users who saw the ad may differ from those who did not in intent, demographics, or prior engagement. Formally, E[Y|T=1] − E[Y|T=0] = ATE + (E[Y(0)|T=1] − E[Y(0)|T=0]), and the second term is non-zero whenever ad exposure is non-random. To make a causal claim you need either (1) an RCT — randomly assign ad exposure so that E[Y(0)|T=1] = E[Y(0)|T=0] by design, or (2) an observational strategy that invokes ignorability — argue that you have measured all variables that determine both who saw the ad and who converted, then control for them via matching, regression, or IPW. The critical question is: is there any variable that predicts ad exposure AND conversion that you have not measured? If yes, ignorability fails and the estimate is biased.`,
      },
      {
        q: `A clinical trial shows a drug has ATE = +5 points on a health scale. A policymaker wants to know the effect of mandating the drug for everyone. Is ATE the right estimand? What if the trial enrolled only volunteers?`,
        a: `If the drug is mandated for everyone, the policy target is ATE over the full population — including people who would not have volunteered. If the trial enrolled volunteers, the estimated ATE may reflect ATT (effect among those who selected into the trial) rather than ATE for the population. Volunteers often differ — they may be more health-conscious, sicker, or more compliant — which means the trial estimate overstates or understates the effect on the general population. The right response is to (1) check covariate distributions of trial participants vs. the target population, (2) consider external validity (how well do trial conditions match real-world use), and (3) if possible, weight the trial estimate toward the target population using covariate reweighting. The failure mode is using a trial ATT as if it were a population ATE.`,
      },
      {
        q: `You run an A/B test for a new social sharing feature. Users in the treatment group can share posts; users in the control group cannot. After the experiment, you find the control group's engagement unexpectedly increased. What is happening and what does it mean for your estimate?`,
        a: `This is a SUTVA violation — specifically, interference between units. Treated users share posts that appear in the feeds of control users, changing control user behaviour even though they did not receive the treatment. The contamination means Y_i(0) for control users is no longer the true baseline outcome, but a spillover-inflated version. The naive estimate underestimates the true effect of the feature because the control group is partially treated. The remedy is cluster-based randomisation — assign clusters (friend groups, geographic regions) to treatment or control so that treated users primarily interact with other treated users and control users interact with other control users, isolating the groups from spillover.`,
      },
      {
        q: `Why can the ignorability assumption never be tested from data? What is the best you can do to support it?`,
        a: `Ignorability says (Y(0), Y(1)) ⊥ T | X — that conditional on X, the potential outcomes are independent of treatment. To test this, you would need to observe both Y(0) and Y(1) for the same unit, which is exactly what the Fundamental Problem prevents. You can never check whether the counterfactual outcomes are balanced across treatment groups because you never observe the counterfactual. The best you can do is (1) check balance on observed covariates — if X is well-balanced, it supports but does not prove ignorability; (2) run placebo tests using outcomes that cannot be causally affected by treatment — if those show effects, something is confounded; (3) argue from the data-generating process that all common causes of T and Y are in X; and (4) conduct sensitivity analysis to quantify how large an unmeasured confounder would need to be to overturn the conclusion.`,
      },
    ],
    takeaway: `Causal inference is a missing data problem — the counterfactual is always absent. Every causal estimate is only as credible as the assumptions used to fill in that missing half. The assumptions are unverifiable from the data used to make the estimate; they must be argued, defended, and subjected to sensitivity analysis.`,
  },
  {
    id: 'dag_confounding',
    title: 'DAGs and Confounding',
    subtitle: 'Directed acyclic graphs, backdoor criterion, collider bias, d-separation',
    difficulty: 'intermediate',
    estimatedMin: 32,
    tags: ['DAG', 'confounding', 'collider bias', 'd-separation'],
    summary: `Controlling for the wrong variable can make things worse, not better. Adjusting for a collider — a variable caused by both treatment and outcome — opens a spurious correlation between treatment and outcome where none existed. Adjusting for a mediator blocks the indirect effect you are trying to measure, causing the model to underestimate the total causal effect. These are not small biases; they can reverse the sign of an estimate. A DAG (Directed Acyclic Graph) makes the causal structure explicit before any regression is run: each node is a variable, each directed arrow is a direct causal claim. The backdoor criterion tells you which variables to condition on to block all spurious paths between treatment and outcome without blocking any causal paths. Without a DAG, you are making these structural decisions implicitly — and making them wrong without any warning that you have done so. Every regression is a causal claim. A DAG makes the claim explicit and checkable.`,
    keyPoints: [
      `**A confounder has arrows into both T and Y: T <- C -> Y.** C creates a backdoor path that produces a spurious association between T and Y even when T has no causal effect on Y. Conditioning on C closes the path and removes the bias. Failing to measure C leaves confounding bias that no amount of data or complex modeling can eliminate — the bias is structural, not statistical.`,
      `**A collider is caused by both T and Y: T -> C <- Y.** Marginally, T and Y have no spurious association through C — the path is blocked. Conditioning on C (including it in a regression, or restricting the sample on it) opens the path and induces a correlation between T and Y that was never causally present. This is collider bias. Including a collider as a control variable in a regression actively makes the estimate worse than not adjusting for it at all.`,
      `**A mediator sits on the causal path: T -> M -> Y.** The mediated path carries part of the total effect of T on Y. Conditioning on M blocks this indirect pathway and gives you only the direct effect — the effect of T on Y through routes that do not go through M. If you want the total effect, do not condition on the mediator. Including engagement metrics as controls when estimating the effect of an algorithm change that operates through engagement is a mediator error.`,
      `**The backdoor criterion gives a precise rule for identifying which variables to adjust for.** A set Z satisfies the backdoor criterion for (T, Y) if Z blocks every path between T and Y that has an arrow into T (backdoor paths), and Z contains no descendant of T (which would block causal paths). When Z satisfies the criterion: E[Y | do(T=t)] = sum_z E[Y | T=t, Z=z] P(Z=z). This recovers the interventional distribution from observational data without randomization.`,
      `**d-separation: X and Y are d-separated by Z if every path between them is blocked.** A non-collider in Z blocks any path through it. A collider not in Z (and with no descendant in Z) is already blocked. A collider in Z opens the path. Adding a "harmless" control variable can introduce bias if it is a collider or a descendant of a collider — d-separation determines whether any given adjustment set is valid.`,
      `**Simpson's Paradox — an association reverses or disappears when conditioning on a third variable — is a symptom of confounding or collider structure.** The resolution requires the DAG. Whether to condition on the third variable depends entirely on its causal role: conditioning on a confounder removes bias; conditioning on a collider introduces it. Looking at which direction of association seems "more correct" is not a valid criterion.`,
      `**Use the minimal sufficient adjustment set — the smallest Z satisfying the backdoor criterion.** Over-adjusting (including variables that are descendants of treatment, or variables that are near-colliders) blocks causal paths, amplifies noise, or introduces bias. Use DAGitty or do-calculus to identify the correct adjustment set before running any regression — do not select control variables based on coefficient significance in the regression output.`,
      `**Build the DAG from mechanism, not from correlation in the data.** List all domain-knowledge common causes of treatment and outcome, draw arrows based on what causes what, check identifiability, then identify the adjustment set. Constructing the DAG after seeing regression results to rationalize a story is post-hoc confabulation. The DAG must be committed to before the analysis begins.`,
    ],
    checkQuestions: [
      {
        q: `You want to estimate the effect of exercise (T) on heart disease (Y). You have data on body weight (W). Draw two plausible DAGs for W and explain what you should do in each case.`,
        a: `DAG 1 — W is a confounder: Diet → W, Diet → Y, Diet → T (diet causes exercise habits, weight, and heart disease). Here W is a common cause of T and Y via diet; you must control for W (or diet) to block the backdoor path. DAG 2 — W is a mediator: T → W → Y (exercise reduces weight which reduces heart disease). Here W is on the causal pathway; controlling for W blocks the indirect effect and gives you only the direct effect of exercise on heart disease not through weight. If you want the total effect, do not control for W. The most realistic DAG is a mix: W is both a mediator and has confounders (Diet → W and T → W → Y simultaneously). In that case, controlling for W blocks the mediated path and introduces bias via conditioning on a node with unmeasured confounders — the correct approach is mediation analysis using the frontdoor criterion or an instrument, not simple regression adjustment.`,
      },
      {
        q: `A researcher conditions on "hospitalisation status" when studying the effect of a drug on mortality. Why might this create collider bias, and what is the consequence for the estimated effect?`,
        a: `Hospitalisation is caused by both illness severity (which also causes mortality) and the decision to take the drug (sicker patients more likely to be hospitalised and treated). So hospitalisation is a collider: Drug → Hospitalisation ← Illness Severity → Mortality. Conditioning on hospitalisation — either by restricting the sample to hospitalised patients or by including it as a control — opens a spurious path between the drug and illness severity within the conditioned sample. Within hospitalised patients, being on the drug is negatively correlated with illness severity (because some patients are there specifically because of the drug, not because they are severely ill). This makes the drug appear more harmful (or less beneficial) than it actually is, which is exactly the bias seen in early COVID observational studies of certain treatments that restricted to hospitalised patients.`,
      },
      {
        q: `What is the difference between the backdoor criterion and the frontdoor criterion, and when would you use the frontdoor criterion?`,
        a: `The backdoor criterion adjusts for common causes of T and Y — it requires measuring the confounders. The frontdoor criterion is used when you cannot measure the confounders but there is a mediator M such that (1) all causal paths from T to Y go through M, (2) there are no unblocked backdoor paths from T to M, and (3) all backdoor paths from M to Y are blocked by T. When these conditions hold, you can identify E[Y | do(T=t)] by adjusting for M even though the T-Y confounders are unmeasured. The classic example is smoking (T) → tar in lungs (M) → cancer (Y) where income/genetics confound smoking and cancer but not the tar-cancer relationship. In practice the frontdoor criterion is rarely applicable because the mediator conditions are strict, but it is valuable precisely because it lets you do causal identification with unmeasured confounding — which the backdoor criterion cannot.`,
      },
      {
        q: `You run a regression of salary on years of experience and performance rating. You find a negative coefficient on performance rating. Should you be alarmed? What DAG structure could explain this?`,
        a: `Yes — this is a sign of collider bias. A plausible DAG: Years of experience → Salary, Performance rating → Salary, and both years of experience and performance contribute to promotion (Salary). If you think of Salary as a collider of experience and performance — both cause salary — then conditioning on Salary (or a proxy like job level) opens a spurious negative path between experience and performance within a fixed salary band. Alternatively, if the sample is restricted to currently employed people, employment is a collider of performance and tenure, and conditioning on employment induces negative correlation. The practical consequence is that the negative coefficient on performance does not mean high performers earn less — it is an artefact of controlling for a variable that is downstream of both predictors. The fix is to reconstruct the causal DAG, identify what role Salary or the conditioning variable plays, and avoid conditioning on colliders.`,
      },
    ],
    takeaway: `Which variables you control for is a causal decision, not a statistical one. Conditioning on a confounder removes bias. Conditioning on a collider introduces it. Conditioning on a mediator blocks the effect you are trying to measure. Draw the DAG before running any regression — the regression cannot tell you which choice was right.`,
  },
  {
    id: 'rct_design',
    title: 'RCT Design',
    subtitle: 'Randomisation, SUTVA, Intent-to-Treat, treatment effect heterogeneity',
    difficulty: 'intermediate',
    estimatedMin: 32,
    tags: ['RCT', 'randomised controlled trial', 'A/B test', 'ITT'],
    summary: `Randomization is the only way to eliminate confounding without any assumption about what you measured. A coin flip deciding who gets treated makes selection bias zero in expectation: who you are is uncorrelated with which side of the coin you landed on. The treatment and control groups are statistically identical on every variable — observed and unobserved — before treatment begins. This is what separates an RCT from every observational method: it does not require you to have measured the right confounders because the randomization makes confounders irrelevant. But randomization is not sufficient. SUTVA violations (network spillover), non-compliance, the wrong unit of randomization, and novelty effects all threaten the estimate even when the coin flip was executed perfectly. A valid A/B test requires deciding the unit of randomization before running, pre-specifying the primary metric, and committing to the analysis plan before looking at results.`,
    keyPoints: [
      `**Randomization guarantees E[Y(0)|T=1] = E[Y(0)|T=0] and E[Y(1)|T=1] = E[Y(1)|T=0] in expectation.** The observed difference E[Y|T=1] - E[Y|T=0] is an unbiased estimator of ATE. No covariate adjustment is necessary for unbiasedness, though controlling for pre-experiment covariates improves precision by reducing residual variance.`,
      `**The unit of randomization determines what SUTVA requires.** User-level randomization is manageable for features without social effects. Session-level randomization allows the same user to see treatment and control in different sessions — carryover contaminates the control group. Cluster-level randomization (geo, friend group) is necessary when one user's treatment would affect others, but inflates variance because within-cluster outcomes are correlated. Power drops dramatically with cluster randomization.`,
      `**SUTVA violations in marketplace experiments: if a pricing tool in treated cities changes supply or demand in ways that spill over to control cities, the control group is contaminated.** The naive estimate conflates the direct effect on treated sellers with spillover effects on supposedly untreated ones. A switchback design (alternating treatment and control across time periods) or geographically isolated holdout clusters partially addresses this.`,
      `**Intent-to-Treat (ITT) compares outcomes by assignment, regardless of whether treatment was actually received.** ITT estimates the effect of the deployment decision — "what happens when we send this email campaign?" — including the fraction of recipients who never open it. ITT is unbiased but attenuated when non-compliance is high. Analyzing only compliant users destroys randomization and reintroduces exactly the selection bias that randomization was designed to eliminate.`,
      `**CACE (Complier Average Causal Effect, also called LATE) is the effect among users who comply with their assignment — who take treatment when assigned and do not take it when not assigned.** CACE = ITT / compliance rate. This requires the monotonicity assumption (no defiers) and uses assignment as an instrument for actual receipt. CACE is more policy-relevant than ITT when you want to know the effect of the feature itself, not the deployment campaign.`,
      `**Power analysis determines the sample size required to detect a meaningful effect with a specified probability. n ≈ 2(z_{alpha/2} + z_beta)^2 sigma^2 / delta^2.** An underpowered test that returns a null result is not evidence of no effect — it is evidence that the test lacked the sensitivity to detect the effect. Pre-registration commits you to the primary metric and analysis plan before looking at data, preventing post-hoc selection of whichever metric happened to be significant.`,
      `**ATE may be zero while subgroup effects are large and opposite in sign, canceling in the aggregate.** Subgroup analysis without pre-specification requires multiple comparisons correction (Bonferroni or FDR) because searching across subgroups inflates Type I error. Subgroup sample sizes are often too small for reliable estimates. The right workflow: confirm an average effect first, then use RCT data with uplift modeling to estimate who benefits most.`,
      `**Novelty effects inflate short-run A/B test estimates.** Users click on new UI elements or engage with new features because they are novel, not because the change is genuinely better. Engagement spikes in the first days, then habituates. Running a test for less than one full weekly cycle captures the novelty spike. Analyze time trends within the test period — if engagement declines consistently over the experiment, the effect estimate includes novelty that will not persist.`,
    ],
    checkQuestions: [
      {
        q: `In your A/B test for a new email feature, 20% of users assigned to treatment never opened the email and thus never saw the feature. You report ITT. What are you estimating, and how would you estimate the effect on those who actually used the feature?`,
        a: `ITT estimates the effect of being assigned to treatment — "what would happen if we deployed this email campaign to the population?" — which includes the 20% who never open emails. The ITT estimate is attenuated toward zero because non-engagers dilute the signal from the 80% who did engage. To estimate the effect on those who would actually use the feature (CACE/LATE), use the ITT estimate and divide by the compliance rate: CACE = ITT / 0.80. This uses treatment assignment as an instrument for actual feature usage. The monotonicity assumption required is that no one in the control group would have used the feature if assigned to treatment (which is trivially true if the feature was not available to control).`,
      },
      {
        q: `You run a marketplace experiment: sellers in treated cities see a new pricing tool; sellers in control cities do not. After two weeks, you see no effect on gross merchandise value (GMV). A colleague says "the test is underpowered — extend it." Another says "there is SUTVA violation — the control cities are contaminated." How do you diagnose which problem you have?`,
        a: `For underpowering: check the pre-specified minimum detectable effect (MDE) and compare it to what you observed. If the confidence interval for the effect is wide and the MDE is not within the interval, power is the issue — extend the test. For SUTVA violation: look for evidence that control cities behaved differently after the experiment started relative to a pre-treatment baseline. If control-city GMV increased during the test period (spillover from treated cities pushing competitors to price differently), that is contamination. Additional diagnostic: check whether the effect estimate changes as you compare cities geographically closer vs. farther from treated cities — stronger contamination signal for close cities would confirm spillover. The right design fix going forward is a switchback experiment (alternating treatment and control over time in all cities) or a holdout geo design with large geographic separation.`,
      },
      {
        q: `Your A/B test shows a statistically significant positive effect on 7-day retention (p=0.02) but you also ran 15 secondary metrics and found significance on 3. How do you interpret this?`,
        a: `With 15 metrics tested at α=0.05, you expect 15 × 0.05 = 0.75 false positives under the null — finding 3 significant results is consistent with multiple testing inflation rather than real effects. The correct approach depends on what was pre-specified: if 7-day retention was the pre-registered primary metric, the p=0.02 result is valid and you can report the secondary metrics as exploratory. If retention was one of 15 metrics without pre-specification, you should apply a correction: Bonferroni gives α_corrected = 0.05/15 = 0.0033 per test, and you should check whether any survive. Alternatively, use Benjamini-Hochberg FDR control if you want to allow some false discoveries. The right practice is always to commit to one primary metric before running the test and treat everything else as hypothesis generation for future experiments.`,
      },
      {
        q: `Why does cluster-level randomisation reduce statistical power, and when is it unavoidable?`,
        a: `Cluster randomisation assigns entire groups (e.g., all users in a city, all users in a friend circle) to the same treatment arm. Within a cluster, user outcomes are correlated — if one person in a city is affected by the treatment, so is every other person in that city. This intra-cluster correlation (ICC) means you effectively have fewer independent observations than the total user count. The variance of the ATE estimate inflates by the design effect: DEFF ≈ 1 + (m − 1) × ICC, where m is cluster size. If ICC=0.1 and clusters have 100 users, DEFF ≈ 10.9 — you need roughly 11× more users to achieve the same power as individual randomisation. Cluster randomisation is unavoidable when SUTVA requires it: social features (treating one user affects friends), marketplace features (supply and demand interact within a geographic region), or any feature where the unit of treatment is the cluster itself (e.g., a new city-level policy). The minimum cluster size for reasonable power is typically in the dozens; with few large clusters (e.g., 5 cities), power is essentially zero regardless of user count.`,
      },
    ],
    takeaway: `Randomization eliminates confounding by construction — but only for the estimand defined by the assignment mechanism. SUTVA violations, non-compliance, and the wrong unit of randomization can each silently invalidate the estimate even when the coin flip was perfectly executed. Designing the experiment correctly matters as much as running it correctly.`,
  },
  {
    id: 'observational_ci',
    title: 'Observational Causal Inference',
    subtitle: 'Propensity score matching, IPW, doubly robust estimators, covariate balance',
    difficulty: 'intermediate',
    estimatedMin: 32,
    tags: ['matching', 'propensity score', 'IPW', 'doubly robust', 'observational studies'],
    summary: `When randomization is impossible — the treatment already happened, it would be unethical, or you are studying a historical policy — observational causal inference tries to recover the causal effect by making treated and control groups comparable using measured covariates. The core strategy is to invoke ignorability: if you measured all variables that jointly determine who received treatment and what outcome they would have had, conditioning on them removes selection bias. Three techniques operationalize this. Matching finds control units that look like each treated unit in covariate space. IPW reweights the sample so the control distribution resembles the treated distribution. Doubly robust estimators (AIPW) combine both and are consistent if either the propensity model or the outcome model is correctly specified — not necessarily both. All three require overlap. And all three can only remove bias from confounders you measured. Unmeasured confounders remain a bias source regardless of how sophisticated the estimator.`,
    keyPoints: [
      `**The propensity score e(X) = P(T=1|X) is the probability of receiving treatment given measured covariates.** The balancing score theorem says that if ignorability holds conditional on X, it also holds conditional on e(X) alone — a single scalar. Matching on the propensity score instead of all of X makes the problem tractable in high dimensions. The score summarizes all covariate information relevant to treatment assignment.`,
      `**PSM procedure: (1) Estimate e(X) via logistic regression on all confounders. (2) Match each treated unit to control units with similar propensity scores. (3) Verify balance: compute SMD = (mean_T - mean_C) / pooled_std for every covariate — SMD < 0.1 is the standard threshold. (4) Estimate ATT in the matched sample.** Skipping the balance check and assuming matching worked produces a biased estimate with no error message — the regression will run and return a number regardless.`,
      `**Balance checking is mandatory, not optional.** Visualize with a Love plot. SMD > 0.1 on a known confounder after matching means the propensity model was misspecified and the matched groups are still systematically different. Remedies: add polynomial and interaction terms to the propensity model, use a tighter caliper, require exact matching on the most important confounders, or switch to entropy balancing which directly optimizes balance rather than going through a propensity score.`,
      `**IPW weights each treated unit by 1/e(X) and each control unit by 1/(1-e(X)).** The weighted sample replicates a pseudo-population where treatment is unconfounded. IPW estimates ATE; PSM estimates ATT. Extreme weights arise when e(X) is near 0 or 1 — a control unit with e(X) = 0.001 gets weight 1,000 and can dominate the entire estimate. Cap weights at the 99th percentile or use stabilized weights to reduce this variance.`,
      `**AIPW (Augmented IPW) is consistent if either the propensity model or the outcome model is correctly specified.** The outcome model predicts what each unit would have received; the IPW correction term removes residual imbalance. One model wrong, the other right: the correction compensates. This double robustness property is why AIPW is the preferred default over simple matching or IPW alone — you need one of the two models to be correct, not both.`,
      `**Overlap is the constraint that every type of unit must have positive probability of being in either group.** When treated units exist in a region of covariate space with no control units, any estimate in that region is pure model extrapolation. Trim to the common support region, estimate ATE for the overlap population only, and explicitly report what was excluded and why. Extrapolating beyond common support produces estimates that depend entirely on model assumptions.`,
      `**Matching discards unmatched units — this reduces variance but introduces bias toward the matched population.** Regression uses all units but is biased when the functional form is wrong and overlap is limited. AIPW or TMLE combines both and is more robust than either alone. For any serious observational analysis where the answer matters, use AIPW or TMLE as the default estimator.`,
      `**Unmeasured confounding is the fatal assumption.** PSM, IPW, and AIPW are unbiased only if ignorability holds — only if all common causes of treatment and outcome are in X. An unmeasured confounder like health consciousness biases the estimate regardless of how well the propensity model fits the observed data. Sensitivity analysis (E-values, Rosenbaum bounds) quantifies how large the unmeasured confounding would need to be to overturn the conclusion — making the residual uncertainty explicit rather than hiding it.`,
    ],
    checkQuestions: [
      {
        q: `After PSM, you check covariate balance and find SMD=0.35 for age — well above the 0.1 threshold. What does this mean and what do you do?`,
        a: `SMD=0.35 means the matched control group is still substantially different from the treated group on age — the matching failed to create comparable groups on this dimension. This directly threatens the causal estimate: age is likely a confounder (older users have different baseline behaviour), so the remaining imbalance introduces residual confounding bias. The remedies, in order: (1) re-specify the propensity model by adding age², age³, or interactions of age with other covariates — the logistic regression may have been too linear to separate users well; (2) use caliper matching with a tight caliper (e.g., 0.01 on the propensity scale) to force closer matches; (3) add exact matching on age quintiles as a hard constraint alongside propensity matching; (4) switch to entropy balancing or CBPS which directly optimise moment conditions for covariate balance rather than trying to balance indirectly through the propensity score. Never report an effect estimate with SMD > 0.1 on a known confounder without flagging the residual bias.`,
      },
      {
        q: `You estimate ATE using IPW. A colleague notices that 5 control observations have weights above 500 while all others are below 20. What is the problem and how do you fix it?`,
        a: `Extreme weights arise when some control units have very small 1 − e(X), meaning e(X) ≈ 1 — the propensity model predicts these control units are almost certain to be treated, so reweighting to make them represent the control population requires a huge weight. This creates a few observations that dominate the weighted estimate, causing high variance and potential bias if those observations are unusual. The problem is near-violation of overlap: these units sit at the edge of the common support region. Fixes: (1) Stabilised weights: replace 1/(1-e(X)) with P(T=0) / (1-e(X)) which anchors the weights to a reasonable scale; (2) Weight trimming: cap weights at the 99th percentile (e.g., 20 in this case) — this introduces small bias but dramatically reduces variance; (3) Common support trimming: exclude units with e(X) above a threshold (e.g., 0.97) entirely — this changes the estimand to the overlap population but removes the problematic region; (4) Use a doubly robust estimator (AIPW) where the outcome model absorbs the burden from extreme propensity scores.`,
      },
      {
        q: `What does "doubly robust" mean in the AIPW estimator? If both the propensity model and the outcome model are misspecified, is the estimate still valid?`,
        a: `Doubly robust means the AIPW estimator is consistent (converges to the true ATE) if either the propensity model OR the outcome model is correctly specified — you only need one of the two to be right, not both. The estimator is: ATE_AIPW = E[μ̂₁(X)] − E[μ̂₀(X)] + E[T(Y − μ̂₁(X))/e(X)] − E[(1-T)(Y − μ̂₀(X))/(1-e(X))]. The first two terms are the outcome model predictions; the last two are IPW correction terms that subtract out whatever the outcome model got wrong. If the outcome model is correct, the correction terms are zero in expectation. If the propensity model is correct, the outcome model errors cancel in expectation. If both are wrong, neither protection activates and the estimate is biased — double robustness is a safety net for one misspecification, not a free pass. In practice, using machine learning models for both (TMLE or DML frameworks) with cross-fitting reduces overfitting bias and provides semiparametric efficiency.`,
      },
      {
        q: `You are studying the effect of a job training program on earnings. Treated individuals self-selected into the program. You have data on age, education, prior earnings, and demographics. You run PSM and find a positive earnings effect. A critic says "there is likely an unmeasured motivation confounder." How do you respond?`,
        a: `The critic is right to raise this: self-selected participants in a job training program are likely more motivated than non-participants, and motivation affects future earnings independently of the program. This unmeasured confounder violates ignorability even after conditioning on all observed covariates. Your response has two parts. First, argue the strength of measured confounders: prior earnings (which partly reflects motivation) and education control for the most observable proxies of motivation; if these are included and balance well, the residual unmeasured motivation confounder may be small. Second, conduct sensitivity analysis: compute the E-value — the minimum association (on the risk ratio scale) that motivation would need to have with both treatment selection and earnings to fully explain the observed effect. If the E-value is 3.0 but domain knowledge suggests motivation-earnings associations are closer to 1.5, the estimate is robust. Report the E-value alongside the main result so readers can judge the credibility of the claim.`,
      },
    ],
    takeaway: `Matching, IPW, and doubly robust estimators all buy you exactly one thing: removal of bias from confounders you measured. The credibility of every observational causal estimate depends entirely on whether you measured the right confounders — not on which estimator you chose. No estimator compensates for an unmeasured confounder.`,
  },
  {
    id: 'iv',
    title: 'Instrumental Variables',
    subtitle: 'Exclusion restriction, weak instruments, 2SLS, LATE',
    difficulty: 'advanced',
    estimatedMin: 30,
    tags: ['IV', 'instrumental variables', '2SLS', 'LATE', 'exclusion restriction'],
    summary: `IV solves a specific problem: you want the causal effect of T on Y, but T is confounded by unobserved variables you cannot measure, so ignorability fails. An instrument Z provides a way around this: Z causally affects T (relevance), has no direct effect on Y and shares no backdoor paths with Y (exclusion restriction), and is uncorrelated with T-Y confounders (independence). IV isolates the variation in T that is driven purely by Z, then uses that clean variation to estimate the T-Y effect.

The result is a consistent causal estimate even with unmeasured confounding — but the price is that the estimate is LATE: the Local Average Treatment Effect for compliers only, the subset of units whose treatment status changes in response to Z. Valid instruments are rare. Exclusion restrictions are contested. Weak instruments produce estimates with confidence intervals so wide they convey essentially no information, and they bias 2SLS toward OLS.`,
    keyPoints: [
      `**IV requires three conditions.** Relevance: Z has a strong first-stage effect on T — first-stage F > 10 is the Staiger-Stock rule of thumb. Exclusion restriction: Z affects Y only through T, with no direct effect on Y and no backdoor paths. Independence: Z is uncorrelated with T-Y confounders. A direct effect of Z on Y (exclusion violation) makes the IV estimate biased — and potentially worse than naive OLS, because the bias now has the wrong sign rather than just being attenuated.`,
      `**2SLS mechanics: Stage 1 — regress T on Z and controls X, obtain predicted values T-hat.** Stage 2 — regress Y on T-hat (not T) and X. Using T-hat isolates the exogenous variation in T driven by Z, discarding the endogenous variation driven by unmeasured confounders. Running OLS in stage 2 on the original T instead of T-hat produces wrong standard errors and wrong confidence intervals even when the coefficient happens to look similar.`,
      `**Weak instruments cause two distinct problems.** First, when Z barely affects T, the first-stage variation is noise-dominated and the resulting IV estimates are unstable — confidence intervals are enormous and individual estimates are unreliable. Second, finite-sample bias toward OLS increases as the first-stage F falls: at F near 1, the 2SLS estimate collapses toward the OLS estimate, defeating the purpose of the instrument. A first-stage F of 4 means the confidence interval is so wide the estimate conveys no actionable information.`,
      `**LATE is what IV estimates: the causal effect for compliers — units who take treatment when Z=1 and do not take it when Z=0.** IV does not estimate ATE (the effect for everyone) or ATT (the effect for those who actually received treatment). Compliers are a latent subgroup you cannot identify directly in the data. Two studies using different instruments for the same treatment T can report different IV estimates — they identify LATE for different complier populations, and the estimates are each correct for their respective complier group.`,
      `**Classic instruments succeed or fail based on the exclusion restriction.** Distance to college affects college attendance (relevance) but should not directly affect earnings — earnings effect must flow through college (exclusion). The challenge: distance also affects local labor market access, peer networks, and parental expectations, each of which could affect earnings directly. Every instrument requires an explicit argument that the exclusion holds, not an assumption that it does.`,
      `**The Hausman test compares OLS and IV estimates.** A significant difference suggests OLS is biased (T is endogenous). If the difference is not significant and the instrument is strong (high F), the null of exogeneity is not rejected. This is a diagnostic — it tells you whether endogeneity is detectable, not whether the exclusion restriction holds. A Hausman test cannot substitute for thinking carefully about the causal mechanism.`,
      `**The over-identification test (Sargan-Hansen) uses the variation across multiple instruments to test whether all instruments jointly satisfy the exclusion restriction.** With more instruments than endogenous variables, if any instrument has a direct effect on Y, the instruments will disagree on the IV estimate. The test catches this case. Caveat: if all instruments violate exclusion in the same direction and by similar amounts, the test has no power to detect it — joint failure is invisible.`,
      `**IV in RCTs with non-compliance: random assignment Z is a valid instrument for actual treatment receipt T — by design, Z is independent of all confounders (randomization) and has no direct effect on Y (randomization only determines assignment, not outcome). 2SLS using Z gives CACE = ITT / compliance rate.** CACE is more policy-relevant than ITT when you want the effect of the treatment mechanism; ITT is more relevant for evaluating the deployment policy.`,
    ],
    checkQuestions: [
      {
        q: `You want to estimate the causal effect of price increases on demand. Propose a valid instrument and explain how you would test its validity.`,
        a: `A valid instrument must affect price (relevance) without directly affecting demand (exclusion). Good candidates: (1) Input cost shocks — a commodity price shock (e.g., oil prices for an airline or trucking company) increases costs and gets passed through to prices, but consumers do not change demand in response to oil prices per se, only in response to the ticket price they observe. (2) Competitor pricing driven by cost-side factors in other markets — a supply shock in competitor's input costs raises their price, which affects your firm's optimal price, but not directly your customers' demand. Testing validity: Relevance — run the first-stage regression of price on the instrument and check F > 10. Exclusion — argue from mechanism: does oil price affect demand through any channel other than the airline's own prices? If consumers react to news about oil prices by anticipating future travel costs, the exclusion is violated. Over-identification test (if multiple instruments): test whether all instruments give the same IV estimate — disagreement suggests at least one violates exclusion. Placebo test: regress the instrument on lagged demand (before any price change) — if the instrument predicts lagged demand, it is correlated with a demand-side confounder.`,
      },
      {
        q: `Your IV estimate of the effect of education on earnings is 15% per year of schooling, but your OLS estimate is 8%. The Hausman test rejects exogeneity. Why might IV be higher than OLS, and what does this tell you about who compliers are?`,
        a: `The IV estimate exceeds OLS, which seems counterintuitive since omitted ability bias should make OLS an overestimate. But IV estimates LATE for compliers — the people who changed their education level in response to the instrument (e.g., people who attended college because there was a college nearby, but would not have attended otherwise). These marginal students — who are on the fence about attending college — may have higher returns to education than the average person. If high-ability students always attend college regardless of distance (never-takers of the no-college state) and low-ability students never attend regardless (always-takers of the college state), then compliers are medium-ability people for whom the marginal year of college has a high return (perhaps they find a specific career path or signalling benefit that non-marginal students already had other access to). So IV > OLS suggests that the returns to education are higher at the margin than the average — policy-relevant because education policy affects marginal students, not the always-attenders.`,
      },
      {
        q: `An economist uses distance to the nearest abortion clinic as an instrument for abortion rates, studying the effect on child outcomes. What are the threats to the exclusion restriction?`,
        a: `The exclusion restriction requires that distance to clinic affects child outcomes only through abortion rates, not through any other pathway. Plausible violations: (1) Geographic sorting — families who live far from clinics may differ in income, religion, political views, or access to healthcare generally; these differences affect child outcomes directly, not through abortion rates. (2) Healthcare access generally — distance to an abortion clinic is correlated with distance to all healthcare facilities in rural areas; lower healthcare access affects child health through prenatal care, childhood vaccinations, etc. (3) Area characteristics — distance is correlated with urbanicity, which predicts outcomes through schools, labour markets, peer effects. The instrument is really measuring rurality, not abortion access specifically. Testing strategy: check whether distance predicts other outcomes it should not affect under exclusion (e.g., adult earnings for children born before the clinics opened, or healthcare utilisation for conditions unrelated to reproductive health). If it does, the exclusion assumption is not plausible.`,
      },
      {
        q: `What is the difference between LATE and ATE, and why does it matter for policy?`,
        a: `ATE is the average treatment effect for the full population: E[Y(1) − Y(0)]. LATE is the average treatment effect for compliers — the subset of the population who change their treatment status in response to the instrument. LATE ≠ ATE unless the treatment effect is homogeneous (the same for everyone). Why it matters for policy: if a job training program is evaluated using a lottery instrument, LATE estimates the effect for lottery-induced participants — people who would not have enrolled without the lottery incentive. If the policy question is "what is the effect of making training mandatory for everyone?", LATE answers a different question — it tells you about a specific subgroup (marginal enrollers), not the always-takers (who would attend regardless) or never-takers (who would not attend even if required). Policymakers sometimes mistakenly cite LATE as justification for a universal mandate, but the effect on people who would never voluntarily enrol may be very different — possibly zero or negative if the training is ineffective for unmotivated participants. Always specify the estimand and the complier population when reporting IV estimates.`,
      },
    ],
    takeaway: `IV trades one untestable assumption (ignorability) for a different untestable assumption (exclusion restriction). The estimate is LATE — the effect for compliers only. A weak instrument does not just add uncertainty; it biases the estimate toward OLS, defeating the purpose. You must argue that the instrument is valid and that the complier population answers the policy question you actually have.`,
  },
  {
    id: 'did',
    title: 'Difference-in-Differences',
    subtitle: 'Parallel trends, event studies, staggered DiD, DiD failures',
    difficulty: 'advanced',
    estimatedMin: 30,
    tags: ['DiD', 'difference-in-differences', 'parallel trends', 'event study', 'staggered DiD'],
    summary: `DiD is the quasi-experimental workhorse when you cannot randomize but have a before/after comparison for both a treated group and a comparable control group. The problem it solves: in observational data, treated cities are always richer (permanent group difference) and the economy changed for everyone (common time trend). The double difference removes both: subtracting the control group's change from the treated group's change leaves only the differential change that appeared exactly when treatment was administered. The critical assumption is parallel trends: in the absence of treatment, treated and control would have followed the same trajectory. This is untestable post-treatment. The best available test is checking pre-treatment trends across multiple periods — if trends diverged before treatment, they would have diverged after regardless of treatment, and the DiD estimate is biased. Recent econometrics has revealed that the standard two-way fixed effects estimator is badly biased in staggered rollout settings, where early-treated units act as controls for later-treated units — producing estimates with the wrong sign when effects are heterogeneous.`,
    keyPoints: [
      `**DiD estimator: delta_DiD = (Y_treated_post - Y_treated_pre) - (Y_control_post - Y_control_pre).** The first difference removes the treated group's pre-treatment baseline. The second difference removes the common time trend shared by both groups. What remains is the differential change attributable to treatment — the only variation consistent with treatment being the cause.`,
      `**Parallel trends is the identifying assumption: in the absence of treatment, treated and control groups would have followed the same trajectory.** Formally: E[Y_treated_post(0) - Y_treated_pre(0)] = E[Y_control_post(0) - Y_control_pre(0)]. Post-treatment this is untestable because Y_treated_post(0) is never observed. Pre-treatment trend checks — showing that the two groups moved together in periods before treatment — provide supporting evidence but do not prove the assumption holds post-treatment.`,
      `**Event study design estimates separate treatment effects for each pre and post period.** Pre-period coefficients should be near zero — that is the parallel trends diagnostic. If pre-period coefficients show a systematic trend (treated group was already diverging before treatment), the DiD estimate is confounded. Post-period coefficients show whether the treatment effect is immediate, builds gradually, or decays over time.`,
      `**Staggered DiD is the setting where units receive treatment at different calendar times.** The standard TWFE estimator in this setting uses early-treated units as implicit controls for later-treated units during the periods when both are treated. The early-treated units' outcomes already include treatment effects — their "control" comparison is contaminated. Callaway-Sant'Anna and Sun-Abraham estimators fix this by restricting the control group to not-yet-treated or never-treated units for each treatment cohort.`,
      `**Common support matters for parallel trends plausibility.** A large city adopting a policy with only small towns as controls makes parallel trends implausible — the two groups were on different economic trajectories before treatment and would remain so regardless. Synthetic control constructs a weighted combination of control units that matches the treated unit's exact pre-treatment trajectory, producing a more credible counterfactual when no single control group is directly comparable.`,
      `**Anticipation effects contaminate the pre-treatment baseline.** If firms start adjusting hiring before a minimum wage law officially takes effect — anticipating the policy change — their pre-treatment employment is already depressed relative to a true no-treatment baseline. Pre-period event study coefficients will deviate from zero, which looks identical to a parallel trends failure. Diagnosing whether the deviation is anticipation or pre-existing trend differences requires domain knowledge.`,
      `**TWFE regression: regress Y on unit fixed effects, time fixed effects, and a treatment indicator.** The treatment coefficient estimates the DiD effect under parallel trends. Include pre-determined covariates or time-varying covariates not affected by treatment. Never include post-treatment covariates — if a covariate is affected by treatment, including it blocks part of the causal pathway and biases the estimate toward zero.`,
      `**In tech, DiD means an A/B test with a pre-period: compare treated and control groups before and after a feature launch.** This is weaker than a pure RCT because parallel trends is an assumption rather than a design guarantee. But when geographically targeted launches or other constraints prevent user-level randomization, DiD is far better than a post-only comparison that cannot distinguish the treatment effect from any pre-existing difference between groups.`,
    ],
    checkQuestions: [
      {
        q: `Your DiD estimate shows a minimum wage increase reduced employment by 3% in treated counties vs control counties. A critic says the two groups had different pre-trends. How do you respond and what would you do?`,
        a: `The critic is raising the most serious threat to DiD validity. Your response: (1) Run the pre-trend test formally. In the TWFE regression, add interactions of the treated group indicator with each pre-treatment time period. These coefficients should all be near zero (and not jointly significant) under parallel trends. Visualise these in an event study plot — if pre-treatment coefficients are large and systematically trending, parallel trends is violated. (2) If pre-trends diverge, standard DiD is biased. Remedies in order of preference: (a) Control for county-level linear time trends (add unit × time interactions) to allow each county to have its own trend — this gives parallel trends conditional on extrapolating pre-treatment trends; (b) Use synthetic control to construct a weighted combination of control counties that matches the treated counties pre-treatment trajectory; (c) Difference-in-difference-in-differences (triple difference) if a third dimension is available (e.g., compare minimum wage effects across counties and across industries that are differentially affected). Report the pre-trend test result alongside the main estimate regardless — its absence from the paper would be a red flag for any reviewer.`,
      },
      {
        q: `You are evaluating a product feature rolled out to different user cohorts in January, March, and May. You plan to use a standard two-period DiD with January as the "treatment group" and March/May as controls. Why is this problematic and what should you use instead?`,
        a: `This is the staggered DiD contamination problem. When you use March and May cohorts as controls for January, you are using units that will eventually be treated — and by the time March receives treatment, the March cohort is no longer a clean control for January. The standard TWFE estimator in staggered settings uses early-treated units as implicit controls for later-treated units and vice versa, which means treatment effects from already-treated units pollute the control estimates. This can produce estimates with the wrong sign if effects are heterogeneous across cohorts. The correct approach: use a staggered-DiD-robust estimator. Callaway-Sant'Anna (2021) constructs clean 2x2 DiD estimates for each cohort using only not-yet-treated or never-treated units as controls, then aggregates across cohorts. Sun-Abraham (2021) implements this within the TWFE framework using interactions. In practice: use the did package in R (Callaway-Sant'Anna) or the csdid Stata package.`,
      },
      {
        q: `A policy that raised fuel efficiency standards was adopted by California in 2005 and by no other state. You want to estimate the effect on vehicle emissions using DiD with other US states as controls. What are the threats to parallel trends, and how would you strengthen the design?`,
        a: `Threats to parallel trends: (1) California is different — it has a unique geography (long coastline, temperate climate), demographics (urban density, income), and political environment. Other states may have had systematically different emissions trends for reasons unrelated to the policy. (2) Anticipation: auto manufacturers and consumers in California may have started adjusting purchasing behaviour before 2005 if the policy was anticipated during legislation, contaminating the pre-period. (3) Simultaneous confounders: California had other environmental regulations (e.g., clean air programs) implemented around the same time. Strengthening the design: (a) Synthetic control — construct a weighted combination of states that matches California pre-2005 on emissions, income, urbanisation, and other predictors; this gives a more credible counterfactual than using all other states equally. (b) Check pre-trends in the event study plot across many years before 2005. (c) Placebo test using outcomes that the fuel efficiency standard should not affect (e.g., water pollution) — if you find effects there, common time shocks are contaminating the estimate. (d) Restrict control states to geographically and economically similar states (Pacific Northwest, Southwest) rather than using all 49.`,
      },
    ],
    takeaway: `DiD identifies causal effects only when parallel trends holds — an assumption that is untestable after treatment. In staggered rollout settings, the standard TWFE estimator is additionally biased even when parallel trends holds for every cohort, because it uses treated units as controls for other treated units. Both the assumption and the estimator choice are core validity questions, not technical details.`,
  },
  {
    id: 'rdd',
    title: 'Regression Discontinuity Design',
    subtitle: 'Sharp and fuzzy RDD, bandwidth selection, manipulation test, local randomisation',
    difficulty: 'advanced',
    estimatedMin: 30,
    tags: ['RDD', 'regression discontinuity', 'bandwidth', 'local randomisation', 'sharp RDD'],
    summary: `RDD exploits a threshold rule: treatment is assigned based on whether a running variable crosses a known cutoff. Students scoring above 70 get a scholarship; stores above a revenue threshold get a different pricing tier. The causal argument is local: near the cutoff, whether you fall just above or just below is essentially random — near-identical units separated by small idiosyncratic measurement noise, not systematic differences in character or behavior. Comparing outcomes just above and just below the cutoff estimates the causal effect without any unmeasured confounders assumption in that narrow window. The price of this high local credibility is narrow external validity: RDD identifies only the treatment effect at the cutoff, for units near the threshold. And the entire design depends on one condition — units cannot precisely manipulate which side of the cutoff they land on. Evidence of manipulation (bunching in the running variable distribution just above the cutoff) invalidates the local randomization assumption.`,
    keyPoints: [
      `**Sharp RDD: treatment is a deterministic step function of the running variable — everyone above cutoff c is treated, no one below.** The estimand is the discontinuity in expected outcomes at the cutoff: tau_RDD = lim_{x down c} E[Y|X=x] - lim_{x up c} E[Y|X=x]. Estimated as the regression discontinuity at c using local polynomial regression on both sides.`,
      `**Fuzzy RDD: treatment probability jumps at the cutoff but is not a complete step function — some below are treated (always-takers), some above are not (never-takers).** Use the cutoff as an instrument for actual treatment receipt: the IV estimate is the discontinuity in Y divided by the discontinuity in P(T=1). This estimates LATE for compliers at the cutoff — units whose treatment status is determined by crossing the threshold.`,
      `**Bandwidth selection is the central technical choice in RDD.** Wide bandwidth uses more data and reduces variance but violates local randomization for units far from the cutoff — those units differ systematically, introducing bias. Narrow bandwidth preserves local randomization but has few observations and high variance. The CCT (Calonico-Cattaneo-Titiunik) optimal bandwidth minimizes MSE by trading bias against variance explicitly. Report estimates at multiple bandwidths — a result that changes dramatically with bandwidth is not a robust finding.`,
      `**The continuity assumption is the core identifying assumption: all other determinants of Y must be continuous functions of the running variable at the cutoff.** Nothing else can jump at the same threshold. This rules out simultaneous policy changes at the same cutoff and manipulation. When the assumption is violated — by something changing discontinuously at the threshold besides the treatment — the RDD estimate conflates the treatment effect with the other discontinuity.`,
      `**The McCrary density test checks for manipulation.** If units can game their score to land just above the cutoff, the density of the running variable will show a spike just above c and a corresponding trough just below. A visible spike in the histogram before the formal test is a red flag. The formal test uses a local polynomial density estimator to test whether the density is continuous at c. Significant bunching means local randomization has been violated by active sorting.`,
      `**Covariate balance tests are the RDD equivalent of DiD pre-trend tests.** Pre-determined covariates — variables that cannot be affected by treatment because they were determined before the running variable was measured — should not jump at the cutoff. Regress each pre-treatment covariate on the running variable and test for a discontinuity at c. Significant covariate jumps indicate that something other than treatment assignment is discontinuous at the threshold.`,
      `**RDD has extremely high internal validity near the cutoff and weak external validity away from it.** Students near the GPA scholarship cutoff are marginal students for whom the scholarship is the deciding factor. Students far above the cutoff would attend college regardless; students far below would not. The scholarship effect for marginal students may be very different from the effect for non-marginal students. Extrapolating the RDD estimate beyond the cutoff requires strong functional form assumptions the design cannot support.`,
      `**Donut RDD excludes observations within a small interval of the cutoff and re-estimates using only units in the donut region.** If the effect persists using only units in [c-20, c-5] and [c+5, c+20], the discontinuity is robust. If the effect disappears when near-cutoff units are excluded, it depended entirely on the observations most vulnerable to manipulation — which is evidence that the result is an artifact of sorting near the cutoff rather than a genuine treatment effect.`,
    ],
    checkQuestions: [
      {
        q: `A university gives scholarships to students who score above 70 on an entrance exam. You want to estimate the effect of the scholarship on graduation rates using RDD. What are your main validity checks?`,
        a: `Four main validity checks: (1) McCrary density test — plot the density of entrance exam scores around 70. If there is a spike just above 70 (students bunching to qualify), some students are manipulating their score, breaking local randomisation. Formal test: run the McCrary density discontinuity test (rddensity package). (2) Covariate balance test — regress pre-determined covariates (family income, high school GPA, demographics) on the running variable and test for a jump at 70. If family income jumps at 70, something else is driving sorting at the cutoff. (3) Bandwidth sensitivity — re-estimate the RDD effect at multiple bandwidths (±5, ±10, ±15 points) and check stability. If the estimate changes dramatically with bandwidth, this signals model sensitivity or violations near the cutoff. (4) Placebo cutoffs — run the RDD at false cutoffs (e.g., 60 and 80) where no policy discontinuity exists. If you find significant effects at placebo cutoffs, the running variable is confounded with other discontinuous processes.`,
      },
      {
        q: `You run a sharp RDD and get a significant treatment effect with bandwidth ±10. With bandwidth ±5 the effect is larger; with bandwidth ±15 it shrinks to near zero. What does this pattern tell you?`,
        a: `This non-monotonic sensitivity pattern is a warning sign. A well-identified RDD should produce estimates that are relatively stable across reasonable bandwidths — the estimate may change slightly as you add noisier observations further from the cutoff, but large swings indicate a problem. Two plausible explanations: (1) Nonlinearity in the running variable-outcome relationship: if the outcome curve is concave near the cutoff, linear extrapolation overestimates the discontinuity with a narrow bandwidth. Use a local quadratic or polynomial fit rather than local linear, and check residual plots. (2) Localised manipulation: units just above the cutoff (within ±5) successfully manipulated their score, and including the wider band dilutes their influence. Check the McCrary density specifically in the ±5 range. The correct response is: (a) plot the RDD graph with confidence intervals at multiple bandwidths, (b) use the CCT optimal bandwidth as your primary specification, (c) report and explain the sensitivity, and (d) investigate the ±5 region specifically for manipulation evidence before drawing conclusions.`,
      },
      {
        q: `A government policy provides business subsidies to firms with revenue below £500k. You want to use RDD to estimate the effect on employment. The McCrary test shows significant bunching just below £500k. Can you still use RDD?`,
        a: `The bunching just below £500k indicates firms are deliberately keeping revenue below the threshold to retain eligibility — this is manipulation. Firms near the cutoff are not randomly assigned to just-above vs just-below; they are sorting. This directly violates the continuity assumption: firms just below £500k are not comparable to firms just above £500k because they made an active choice to be there. In this form, standard RDD is invalid near the cutoff. However, there are partial remedies: (1) Donut RDD — exclude the bunching region (e.g., £475k–£500k where manipulation is concentrated) and estimate the effect using firms in a donut around the cutoff. Firms at £450k–£475k (just below the donut) vs £500k–£525k (just above) may not have been able to manipulate as precisely. This changes the estimand to the effect at the donut boundary. (2) Model the manipulation explicitly — use a "bunching" estimator that models the counterfactual distribution absent manipulation and estimates the treatment effect from the distribution shape. (3) Acknowledge in the write-up that RDD provides only a lower bound if manipulation is asymmetric (firms below the threshold do not reduce revenue to just below; they do, which means estimates are attenuated).`,
      },
    ],
    takeaway: `RDD achieves credible causal identification near the cutoff without any unmeasured confounders assumption — because local randomization near the threshold eliminates the need to measure confounders. But this credibility is strictly local: it covers only units near the cutoff, only when those units did not manipulate their running variable, and only if nothing else changes discontinuously at the same threshold. High internal validity in a narrow window; external validity requires extrapolation the design cannot support.`,
  },
  {
    id: 'uplift_modeling',
    title: 'Uplift Modeling',
    subtitle: 'S-learner, T-learner, X-learner, R-learner — heterogeneous treatment effects and targeting',
    difficulty: 'advanced',
    estimatedMin: 35,
    tags: ['uplift', 'CATE', 'causal ML', 'treatment effect', 'meta-learner'],
    summary: `Sending a discount to every high-propensity customer is the standard mistake: it identifies people likely to buy, not people who buy because of the discount. The Sure Things buy regardless — you spent the discount margin for nothing. The Do-Not-Disturbs were going to buy and now feel pressured — treatment backfires. Uplift modeling targets a different question: not "who is likely to convert?" but "for whom does treatment increase conversion?" CATE — the Conditional Average Treatment Effect tau(x) = E[Y(1) - Y(0) | X=x] — is the expected causal lift for an individual with features x. You cannot observe tau(x) directly because you never observe both Y(1) and Y(0) for the same person. Meta-learners (S, T, X, R-learner) estimate tau(x) from RCT data using prediction models. Standard accuracy metrics (RMSE, AUC) cannot measure uplift performance because the truth is never observed — use Qini or AUUC evaluated on a held-out RCT split.`,
    keyPoints: [
      `**Four response types define who to target.** Persuadables (Y(1)=1, Y(0)=0): respond only because of treatment — these are the correct targets. Sure Things (Y(1)=1, Y(0)=1): would have converted regardless — discount is wasted margin. Lost Causes (Y(1)=0, Y(0)=0): treatment has no effect. Do-Not-Disturbs (Y(1)=0, Y(0)=1): would have converted without treatment but do not convert when treated — treatment actively backfires. A response model that ranks by P(Y=1) targets Sure Things and Do-Not-Disturbs disproportionately, wasting budget and potentially reducing net revenue.`,
      `**S-learner trains a single model on all features including the treatment indicator: {X, T} -> Y.** To estimate CATE, compute y-hat(x, T=1) - y-hat(x, T=0). Simple and uses all data. Failure mode: regularization shrinks the treatment coefficient toward zero when the treatment indicator is low-signal relative to X features. Tree models may never split on T at all, producing tau-hat(x) near zero everywhere — systematically underestimating heterogeneity.`,
      `**T-learner trains two separate models: mu-hat_1(x) on treated units and mu-hat_0(x) on controls.** Estimate tau-hat(x) = mu-hat_1(x) - mu-hat_0(x). Treatment effects are not regularized away because T never enters the models. Failure mode: when treatment and control groups are severely imbalanced (90% control, 10% treated), mu-hat_1 is trained on few examples — high variance — and errors from both models compound in the subtraction.`,
      `**X-learner addresses T-learner's imbalance problem.** Step 1: T-learner produces mu-hat_0, mu-hat_1. Step 2: impute individual treatment effects — D-tilde = mu-hat_1(x) - Y for controls, D-tilde = Y - mu-hat_0(x) for treated. Step 3: train tau-hat_0 on controls and tau-hat_1 on treated. Step 4: tau-hat(x) = e(x)*tau-hat_1(x) + (1-e(x))*tau-hat_0(x). The imputation step borrows strength from the larger group, dramatically reducing variance when groups are imbalanced. Default for observational data with 90/10 splits.`,
      `**R-learner estimates nuisance functions e(X) = P(T=1|X) and m(X) = E[Y|X], then minimizes the loss: sum[(Y_i - m-hat(X_i)) - tau(X_i)(T_i - e-hat(X_i))]^2.** Orthogonalization means first-order errors in e and m do not propagate into tau-hat(X) — this is the Neyman orthogonality property. R-learner achieves minimax-optimal CATE estimation rates and is the recommended default in EconML for high-stakes applications where bias in the nuisance functions would otherwise distort CATE estimates.`,
      `**Uplift evaluation cannot use RMSE because tau(x) is never directly observed.** The Qini coefficient measures the area between the uplift curve (cumulative incremental outcomes versus fraction of population targeted, ranked by tau-hat descending) and the random targeting baseline. Qini > 0 means targeting by estimated uplift outperforms random targeting. Critical limitation: Qini measures ranking quality, not calibration. A model can rank correctly (high tau-hat users actually have higher treatment effects) while systematically overestimating the magnitude of those effects.`,
      `**CATE estimation from observational data inherits all the identification problems of ATE estimation — and adds new ones.** Ignorability must hold within every subgroup defined by X, not just on average. Overlap must hold throughout X-space, not just marginally. A region of X where all units are treated means tau-hat(x) in that region is pure model extrapolation. Reporting observational CATE estimates as causal targeting without checking these conditions is false precision.`,
      `**Targeting policy: rank by tau-hat(x) descending, target the top fraction where expected incremental revenue exceeds the cost of the intervention.** Targeting the top decile by uplift versus top decile by response probability typically yields 2-5x higher incremental conversion per dollar in real email campaigns. Post-deployment validation: run a small holdout RCT within the targeted population to verify that predicted uplift matches actual lift. This is the only way to confirm the model is measuring causal heterogeneity rather than baseline propensity differences.`,
    ],
    checkQuestions: [
      {
        q: `Your response model targets customers with the highest predicted purchase probability. Why might this be suboptimal? How would uplift modeling change the targeting strategy?`,
        a: `The response model identifies customers likely to purchase — but "likely to purchase" is not the same as "likely to purchase because of the treatment." High-baseline customers are often Sure Things: they would have purchased regardless of the discount. Sending them a 20% discount erodes margin without incrementally driving a purchase. Worse, some high-probability customers may be Do-Not-Disturbs — loyal customers who are annoyed by discount emails and eventually unsubscribe. Uplift modeling estimates τ̂(x) = E[Y(1) − Y(0) | X=x] — the incremental purchase probability caused by the discount — and targets users where this is highest, not where baseline probability is highest. In practice, persuadables are often mid-propensity users (not too likely, not too unlikely to purchase) who are genuinely swayed by an offer. The Qini curve captures this: at any given budget, targeting by τ̂(x) achieves more incremental purchases than targeting by ŷ(x), and the difference is often 2–5× in real-world email campaigns.`,
      },
      {
        q: `You have an RCT dataset with 100,000 control users and 10,000 treated users. You want to estimate CATE. Which meta-learner would you use and why? What failure modes should you watch for?`,
        a: `With a 10:1 control-to-treatment imbalance, use the X-learner. The T-learner is problematic here: μ̂₁(x) is trained on only 10,000 treated observations, making it high-variance in sparse covariate regions — and τ̂(x) = μ̂₁(x) − μ̂₀(x) compounds the noise from μ̂₁. The X-learner's imputation step — computing D̃ = Ŷ(1) − Y_observed for control units using the treated model — borrows from the 100,000 control observations to stabilise estimates. The propensity-weighted combination τ̂(x) = e(x)τ̂₁(x) + (1−e(x))τ̂₀(x) further down-weights the noisy treated-side estimate (e(x) is small everywhere given 10% treatment rate). Failure modes to watch: (1) The X-learner inherits T-learner's μ̂₁ — check its fit quality on the 10,000 treated observations via cross-validated RMSE; if μ̂₁ is poor, the imputed ITEs for controls are biased. (2) The propensity score e(x) at 0.1 everywhere makes the weighting collapse to τ̂₀(x) — verify that the treated τ̂₁(x) sub-model is not ignored entirely in regions where it carries useful signal. (3) Validate with Qini on a held-out RCT test set; if Qini ≈ 0, the CATE estimates are not ranking individuals by true uplift.`,
      },
      {
        q: `After training an X-learner for marketing targeting, how would you evaluate whether the CATE estimates are actually measuring causal heterogeneity versus spurious correlation?`,
        a: `Four-part validation strategy: (1) Qini / AUUC on held-out RCT data — split the RCT data into train/test. Train the X-learner on train, rank test set by τ̂(x). Plot the uplift curve: cumulative incremental outcomes (treated − control within the targeting percentile) as you target more people. If τ̂(x) is measuring real causal heterogeneity, the curve should rise steeply in the high-τ̂ group and flatten or turn negative in the low-τ̂ group. A flat Qini curve means the model is not discriminating on causal heterogeneity. (2) Subgroup lift verification — for top and bottom decile by τ̂(x), estimate actual ITEs within each decile using the holdout RCT (treated minus control within the decile). If the top decile has significantly higher actual ITT than the bottom decile, the ranking is capturing real heterogeneity. (3) Plausibility check — the features driving τ̂(x) should make domain sense. If "email open rate" is the top feature driving τ̂, that is intuitive (engaged users respond to email). If "user account creation date" is the top driver with no plausible mechanism, it may be overfitting to noise. (4) Post-deployment holdout — deploy the targeting policy to 90% of users, keep a 10% random holdout. Compare conversion in targeted-by-uplift vs random groups in the holdout to measure real-world lift.`,
      },
      {
        q: `What is the R-learner and why does "orthogonalisation" matter for CATE estimation?`,
        a: `The R-learner is based on the observation that Y − m(X) = τ(X) × (T − e(X)) + ε, where m(X) = E[Y|X] and e(X) = P(T=1|X). Rearranging, τ(X) ≈ (Y − m(X)) / (T − e(X)), which says: after partialling out the baseline outcome and the propensity, the residual treatment-response relationship is the CATE. In practice this is solved by minimising the loss: Σᵢ [(Yᵢ − m̂(Xᵢ)) − τ(Xᵢ)(Tᵢ − ê(Xᵢ))]². Orthogonalisation matters because of Neyman orthogonality: the gradient of the loss with respect to τ is zero when m and e are at their true values, which means first-order errors in estimating m and e do not bias the CATE estimate. This is the key failure mode of non-orthogonal approaches: if e(X) is estimated with error and you do not orthogonalise, that error propagates into τ̂(X) via confounding — the estimated CATE picks up variation due to propensity misspecification. Orthogonalisation, combined with cross-fitting (estimating m and e on a separate fold), gives semiparametrically efficient and essentially unbiased CATE estimates even when m and e are estimated with flexible ML models that would otherwise overfit and bias the main estimate.`,
      },
      {
        q: `You estimate CATE using an X-learner on observational data (no RCT). A colleague argues the estimates cannot be trusted. Who is right?`,
        a: `The colleague is mostly right. CATE from observational data inherits all the identification problems of ATE estimation from observational data — and then some. For ATE, ignorability (Y(0), Y(1)) ⊥ T | X is already an untestable assumption. For CATE estimation, you need ignorability to hold within every subgroup defined by X — and you need sufficient overlap within every subgroup (not just overall). Thin regions of X-space may have near-zero overlap, making CATE estimates in those regions pure extrapolation. Additionally, the meta-learners require that the outcome model fits well in both treatment and control groups within every subgroup — a harder requirement than fitting well on average. The defence of observational CATE estimates: (1) argue ignorability from domain knowledge — did you measure all confounders? (2) check overlap throughout X-space, not just on marginal distributions; (3) run sensitivity analysis (vary the assumed confounding level and check how τ̂(x) changes); (4) where possible, validate by running a small RCT on a subset and checking whether the observational CATE matches the RCT CATE within the tested subgroups. If the comparison fails, the observational estimates are biased.`,
      },
    ],
    takeaway: `A response model finds people likely to convert. An uplift model finds people who convert because of your treatment. Targeting the first group wastes budget on Sure Things and backfires on Do-Not-Disturbs. A CATE estimate is only as trustworthy as the identification strategy behind it, and a Qini curve without a holdout RCT validation is not evidence of causal targeting — it is evidence of ranking, which may or may not reflect actual treatment effect heterogeneity.`,
  },
  {
    id: 'mediation',
    title: 'Mediation Analysis',
    subtitle: 'Direct vs indirect effects, NDE/NIE decomposition, sequential ignorability',
    difficulty: 'advanced',
    estimatedMin: 30,
    tags: ['mediation', 'indirect effects', 'NDE', 'NIE', 'causal path analysis'],
    summary: `Knowing that a treatment works is less useful than knowing how it works. If a new recommendation algorithm raises revenue, the mechanism matters: if 90% of the effect runs through session length, invest in session length features; if 90% runs through click-through rate, invest differently. Mediation analysis decomposes the total causal effect into Natural Direct Effect (NDE: T affects Y through pathways that do not go through M) and Natural Indirect Effect (NIE: T affects Y by first changing M). The technical problem is that mediation requires sequential ignorability — T must be ignorable given X, and M must be ignorable given (T, X). The first condition is guaranteed by a well-run RCT. The second is not. M is a post-treatment variable and can be confounded by unmeasured variables that affect both M and Y. Even in a perfect experiment, the mediation decomposition rests on an additional unverifiable assumption. Sensitivity analysis for unmeasured mediator-outcome confounding is not optional.`,
    keyPoints: [
      `**TE = NDE + NIE.** NDE = E[Y(1, M(0))] - E[Y(0, M(0))]: the effect of T on Y if the mediator were held at its control-world value — "what if treatment changed but M did not follow?" NIE = E[Y(1, M(1))] - E[Y(1, M(0))]: the effect of M changing from its control to treatment value, holding T=1 — "how much of the total effect runs through M?" These potential outcomes involve cross-world quantities — values from both the treatment and control worlds simultaneously — which is why they require stronger assumptions than total effect estimation.`,
      `**Sequential ignorability requires two conditions.** First: T must be ignorable given X — an RCT guarantees this. Second: M must be ignorable given (T, X) — an RCT does NOT guarantee this. M is measured after treatment. Unmeasured variables that affect both M and Y (but were not caused by T) bias NDE and NIE even in a well-run experiment. An RCT solves the first half of the identification problem. The second half must be argued.`,
      `**Baron-Kenny linear mediation: (1) confirm total effect (regress Y on T), (2) confirm T -> M (regress M on T), (3) regress Y on T and M — if T's coefficient drops, M mediates.** Sobel test: indirect effect = alpha * beta_M. These steps are intuitive and widely used. They assume linearity and sequential ignorability simultaneously. Under nonlinear relationships or when sequential ignorability fails, these steps give wrong decompositions — often confidently wrong.`,
      `**Proportion mediated = NIE / TE.** The interpretation pitfall: this can exceed 1 when NDE and NIE have opposite signs (suppressor effects), or be negative. A negative proportion mediated is mathematically valid but communicatively confusing. Report NDE and NIE directly, not just their ratio — the ratio loses sign information and the absolute effect sizes.`,
      `**Including M in a total effect regression blocks the indirect pathway and underestimates the total causal effect of T.** Worse: if M is caused by both T and an unmeasured confounder U of the M-Y relationship, conditioning on M opens a collider-like spurious path between T and U. The regression coefficient for T then reflects a mix of the direct effect and a spurious path through the collider. Decide the estimand (total effect or direct effect?) before running any regression, then use the DAG to determine what to condition on.`,
      `**Sensitivity analysis for mediation quantifies how large an unmeasured mediator-outcome confounder would need to be to reverse the mediation conclusion.** VanderWeele's sensitivity parameter asks: if an unmeasured variable U has odds ratio gamma_A with the mediator and gamma_Y with the outcome, at what values of these parameters does the indirect effect change sign? If the conclusion reverses at gamma = 1.5 and known confounders have gamma around 2, the finding is fragile. Report this sensitivity parameter alongside every mediation estimate.`,
      `**Multiple mediators decompose into separate indirect effects only when the mediators are causally independent — no M_1 -> M_2 path.** If M_1 causes M_2, conditioning on M_2 when estimating the M_1 pathway blocks part of M_1's indirect effect that runs through M_2. The resulting estimate conflates the M_1-direct indirect effect with the M_1->M_2->Y pathway. Sequential mediation analysis with the full causal graph is required when mediators have causal relationships with each other.`,
      `**In A/B test attribution: a new feed algorithm increases 30-day LTV by some amount.** Mediation analysis allocates that LTV gain across session length and notification click-through. If 90% runs through session length, prioritizing session-length features is the right next step. The caveat: this attribution requires sequential ignorability for each mediator — measure and condition on all variables that jointly determine each mediator's value and the outcome after treatment. Failing to measure a mediator confounder produces an incorrect attribution that points engineering effort in the wrong direction.`,
    ],
    checkQuestions: [
      {
        q: `A new recommendation algorithm (T) increases 7-day retention (Y) by 5 percentage points. You suspect the mechanism is increased session length (M). Describe a valid mediation analysis including the assumptions and failure modes.`,
        a: `Mediation analysis steps: (1) Confirm total effect: compare 7-day retention between treatment and control using the A/B data. Total effect = 5pp (given). (2) Estimate T → M: compare average session length between treatment and control — is the algorithm significantly increasing session length? (3) Estimate direct and indirect effects: regress retention on T and M jointly. Does the coefficient on T decrease when M is included? Use the mediation package (R: mediation; Python: causalml) to estimate NIE = effect through session length, NDE = remaining direct effect. Bootstrap confidence intervals for NIE. Assumptions: (1) RCT guarantees T ignorability. (2) Sequential ignorability for M: there must be no variable that affects both session length and retention independently of T. For example, if user motivation affects both how long users stay in a session AND whether they return next week (and motivation is not changed by the algorithm), this is an unmeasured mediator-outcome confounder — even in the RCT. Failure modes: (1) If session length is a collider (unlikely here but check the DAG), conditioning on it opens spurious paths. (2) Non-linear mediation: if the session-retention relationship is non-linear, Baron-Kenny understates NIE in low-engagement segments. Use the non-parametric mediation approach (VanderWeele). (3) Reverse causation: does retention affect session length (users who retained simply have more sessions)? This would violate the mediator-outcome temporal ordering.`,
      },
      {
        q: `You control for a variable M in a regression of Y on T, and the coefficient on T drops from 0.4 to 0.1. Your colleague concludes "80% of the effect of T on Y is mediated by M." What questions would you ask before accepting this conclusion?`,
        a: `Four critical questions: (1) Is M actually a mediator (T → M → Y) or a confounder (M → T and M → Y)? If M is a confounder, the drop in the T coefficient reflects removal of confounding bias, not mediation — the "80% mediated" interpretation is wrong. Draw the causal DAG before interpreting any coefficient change. (2) Does M satisfy sequential ignorability? Are there unmeasured variables that affect both M and Y? If yes, the regression of Y on T and M has residual omitted variable bias for the direct effect, inflating or deflating the apparent NDE. (3) Is M a collider or descendant of T and a confounder of M-Y? If M is a collider, conditioning on it opens a spurious path and the coefficient change is an artefact, not a mediated effect. (4) Is the linear model appropriate? Baron-Kenny assumes linear additive effects — if T interacts with M in its effect on Y, the proportion mediated is not the right summary. Use the VanderWeele interaction decomposition instead, which separates out controlled direct effects, reference interaction, mediated interaction, and pure indirect effect.`,
      },
    ],
    takeaway: `Mediation requires a stronger identification assumption than total effect estimation. An RCT guarantees T-ignorability but not M-ignorability — the mediator is post-treatment and can be confounded by unmeasured variables. Every mediation result should be accompanied by sensitivity analysis for unmeasured mediator-outcome confounding. Without it, you are presenting a mechanism decomposition that cannot be defended.`,
  },
  {
    id: 'sensitivity_analysis',
    title: 'Sensitivity Analysis for Causal Claims',
    subtitle: 'E-values, Rosenbaum bounds, placebo tests, falsification design',
    difficulty: 'advanced',
    estimatedMin: 30,
    tags: ['sensitivity analysis', 'E-value', 'Rosenbaum bounds', 'placebo test', 'robustness'],
    summary: `Sensitivity analysis is how you honestly report the strength of a causal claim. The core identification assumptions — ignorability, parallel trends, exclusion restriction — are untestable from the data used to estimate the effect. Sensitivity analysis does not test them. It quantifies how badly they would have to be violated to change the conclusion. An estimate that stands against a hidden confounder with a risk ratio of 10 is far more credible than one that collapses at 1.2 — even though both have the same p-value. Placebo tests provide indirect positive evidence: they check observable implications that would be violated if the assumption were false. If the test passes, the assumption is not proven; if it fails, the assumption is challenged. Together, sensitivity analyses and placebo tests are the honest wrapper around any causal claim. Not "the effect is X" but "the effect is X, and here is how much you would need to distrust the design to disbelieve it."`,
    keyPoints: [
      `**Rosenbaum bounds apply to matching-based studies.** They parameterize the maximum odds ratio Gamma of unmeasured confounding: how much more likely could treated units be to receive treatment than equally observable control units? The test asks: at what Gamma does the significance disappear? Sensitive at Gamma=1.2 means fragile — a confounder that doubles the odds of treatment by just 20% overturns the conclusion. Robust to Gamma=3 means credible. Report the Gamma at which significance breaks as a required part of any matching-based causal claim.`,
      `**The E-value is the minimum risk ratio an unmeasured confounder would need simultaneously with both treatment receipt and outcome to fully explain away the observed effect.** E-value = RR + sqrt(RR * (RR - 1)). An E-value of 3 means a single unmeasured confounder would need to triple the odds of treatment and triple the odds of the outcome simultaneously. Compare against known confounders: if the most plausible unmeasured variable has an association of RR=1.4 and the E-value is 3.0, it cannot explain the result away — even if the confounder exists.`,
      `**Placebo outcome test: run the same causal analysis substituting an outcome that T logically cannot affect.** If estimating the effect of a pricing policy on retention, test the same design on customer zip code or account creation date. A significant effect on the placebo signals confounding — something correlated with the policy is also correlated with pre-existing customer characteristics, producing a spurious association in the main analysis.`,
      `**Placebo treatment tests use pre-treatment periods as a falsification check.** In DiD, this means testing whether there is a "treatment effect" in periods before the treatment began — event study coefficients should cluster near zero. In IV, this means permuting instrument assignment randomly and verifying the resulting IV estimate is near zero. A large placebo treatment effect signals that the design is capturing pre-existing differences, not a causal effect.`,
      `**Donut RDD robustness excludes observations within a small range of the cutoff and re-estimates using only the donut region.** If the effect persists using units in [c-20, c-5] and [c+5, c+20], the discontinuity is real and not driven by manipulation near the boundary. If the effect disappears when near-cutoff units are excluded, the result depended entirely on the observations most susceptible to gaming — evidence that the design is invalid near the cutoff.`,
      `**Equivalence testing for covariate balance: a standard t-test that fails to reject H_0: SMD=0 is weak evidence of balance — any underpowered test produces this result trivially.** TOST (Two One-Sided Tests) with bounds ±0.1 provides positive evidence that SMD is within a meaningful range. The distinction matters: "failed to reject imbalance" is not the same as "demonstrated balance." Regulatory submissions and causal papers should use equivalence testing, not the absence of significant imbalance.`,
      `**Negative control outcomes and exposures detect unmeasured confounding.** A negative control outcome is one T logically cannot affect — if significant effects appear, confounding is driving the result. A negative control exposure is one that cannot affect Y — if significant effects appear on Y, the exposure variable has confounded associations. Calibrating the estimated bias using negative controls where the true effect is known to be zero allows you to adjust the main estimate for the estimated confounding level.`,
      `**Present sensitivity analyses alongside main results as a package: the main point estimate, the E-value or Rosenbaum Gamma, placebo test results, and estimates across a range of specification choices.** A result that holds across all of these is a credible causal claim. A result that collapses under minor sensitivity analysis should be presented as suggestive, not conclusive. The difference between a credible and a speculative causal claim is this honest accounting of fragility.`,
    ],
    checkQuestions: [
      {
        q: `Your matching analysis shows that a new treatment increases survival rates by 30% (RR=1.3). A critic says there may be unmeasured confounders. You compute an E-value of 1.9. What does this mean and how do you use it?`,
        a: `E-value = 1.9 means that for an unmeasured confounder to fully explain away the observed RR=1.3, it would need to have a risk ratio of at least 1.9 with both treatment receipt and survival — simultaneously. To contextualise this: look at known confounders in the literature. If the most plausible unmeasured confounder (e.g., socioeconomic status) has a known association of around RR=1.4 with both treatment access and survival, then 1.4 < 1.9 — a single unmeasured SES confounder would not fully explain the result, though it could attenuate it. If smoking has RR=2.5 with survival and is also likely associated with treatment receipt, and smoking was not measured, then 2.5 > 1.9 — smoking alone could explain the entire effect away. The E-value is a communication tool: it converts "might there be confounders?" into "how strong would a confounder need to be?" — which is a specific enough question to answer with domain knowledge.`,
      },
      {
        q: `Your DiD estimate shows a minimum wage increase reduced employment by 3% in treated counties versus control counties. A critic says treated and control counties had different pre-trends. How do you test and respond to this?`,
        a: `Formal pre-trend test: in the TWFE regression, add interaction terms T_i × 1(t = pre-period) for each pre-treatment period. If parallel trends holds, these pre-treatment interaction coefficients should all be near zero and jointly insignificant. Plot them in an event study diagram — both magnitude and direction of pre-period coefficients matter. If the pre-trends are diverging systematically (e.g., treated counties were already declining in employment before the minimum wage), the DiD assumption is violated and the −3% estimate is biased upward (the treatment counties would have declined even without the policy). Remedies if pre-trends diverge: (1) Add county-specific linear time trends to the regression — this controls for county-level pre-trends by extrapolating them forward, and identifies the DiD from the deviation from each county's own trend. (2) Synthetic control — construct a weighted combination of control counties that matches the treated counties pre-treatment trajectory exactly, then compare post-treatment outcomes to that synthetic counterfactual. (3) Report the pre-trend test result honestly in the paper — a critic making this claim is doing exactly what good causal inference requires.`,
      },
      {
        q: `You want to test whether an IV analysis for the effect of college education on earnings (using college proximity as instrument) is confounded. Describe two falsification tests you would run.`,
        a: `Falsification test 1 — Pre-instrument outcome placebo: if college proximity is truly exogenous, it should not predict earnings for individuals who were too old to benefit from nearby colleges at the time of their schooling decisions. Run the same IV analysis on a cohort that was already in the workforce before the relevant colleges were built or before proximity was meaningfully varying. A significant "effect" for these older cohorts (who could not have been affected by proximity during their schooling years) would signal that proximity is correlated with local labour market conditions that affect earnings directly — a violation of the exclusion restriction. Falsification test 2 — Pre-determined covariate test: regress pre-determined covariates (parents' education, family income from a survey conducted before the individual's schooling decision) on the instrument (college proximity). If proximity predicts pre-determined family characteristics, it is not exogenous — it is correlated with geographic sorting of families, which creates a direct pathway from proximity to earnings via family human capital rather than through the individual's own education. Both tests do not prove exogeneity if they pass, but they provide positive evidence and identify the most plausible specific exclusion violations.`,
      },
      {
        q: `After reporting a positive causal effect of a product feature on revenue using DiD, a sceptical executive asks: "How fragile is this result? If you had made different modelling choices, would it still hold?" How do you answer rigorously?`,
        a: `This is a specification robustness or multiverse analysis question. The rigorous answer is to construct a specification curve: (1) Bandwidth / window — vary the pre-post window (3 months vs 6 months vs 12 months) and report estimates for each. If the effect is stable across windows, it is not an artefact of a particular window. (2) Control group definition — try different control group constructions (same geography, similar-sized markets, synthetic control). Report how the estimate changes. (3) Outcome metric — run the analysis on related but distinct outcome metrics (revenue per user, transaction count, gross margin). If the effect is real, it should appear in mechanistically related outcomes. (4) Regression specification — with covariates vs without, with unit-specific trends vs without, with different clustering levels for standard errors. (5) Placebo test summary — report the distribution of placebo estimates (using pre-treatment pseudo-treatment dates) and show where the main estimate sits in that distribution — if it is at the 97th percentile, the effect is unlikely to be a statistical artefact. Present all of these in a figure showing the range of estimates across specifications. If the effect is consistently positive and significant across the specification curve, the executive has strong grounds to trust it.`,
      },
    ],
    takeaway: `Sensitivity analysis does not rescue a weak design — it honestly reports how much trust the design deserves. A small E-value and a failed placebo test means weak evidence regardless of statistical significance. A large E-value and passing falsification tests means strong evidence even without perfect identification. The choice to omit sensitivity analysis is a choice to hide the fragility of the claim.`,
  },
]
